 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Ratings and pulse shape",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The 600 W rating and every IPP figure are for one 10/1000 µs pulse: a 10 µs rise to the peak and a decay to half the peak at 1000 µs (Fig. 3). Shorter pulses allow more power, about 30 kW at 0.1 µs, falling to 0.5 kW at 1 ms (Fig. 1). Above 25 °C ambient, pulse power and current fall linearly to zero at 150 °C (Fig. 2). Steady-state dissipation of 5.0 W holds to 50 °C and reaches zero at 150 °C (Fig. 5)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uni- and bidirectional parts",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Each voltage comes in two forms: the A suffix is unidirectional, with a cathode band, and the CA suffix is bidirectional with no band. Forward voltage and forward surge current apply to unidirectional parts only. Bidirectional parts up to 10 V stand-off have twice the listed leakage limit. At zero bias a unidirectional part has the higher junction capacitance; capacitance falls with breakdown voltage and with reverse bias (Fig. 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage and failure",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Typical leakage is below 5 µA for parts above 12 V. When overstressed in voltage or current the part typically fails short."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and materials",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Glass-passivated junction; moulding compound rated UL 94V-0; matte tin, lead-free plating; halogen free and RoHS compliant. Terminals withstand 260 ℃ for 40 s in soldering. Whisker testing follows JEDEC JESD201A, tables 4a and 4c."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Rated 30 kV air discharge and 30 kV contact discharge to IEC 61000-4-2."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal mounting",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The pulse power rating assumes each terminal on a 5.0 mm x 5.0 mm copper pad, 0.03 mm thick. Thermal resistance is 20 °C/W junction to lead and 100 °C/W junction to ambient, typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Intended for transient suppression on I/O lines, VCC buses and similar sensitive nodes; markets named are telecom, computing, industrial and consumer electronics; the surges named are lightning-induced and other voltage transients."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Part number",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "SMBJ is the series; the number that follows is VRWM in volts; C marks a bidirectional part; A marks 5 % VBR tolerance. For example SMBJ22A is unidirectional and SMBJ22CA bidirectional."
  }
